course: dictionary
lesson: 13914
title: Lawfully: words about rules and the law
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lawfully is an adverb. It comes from the adjective lawful, which means allowed by the law. The -ly on the end tells you it describes how something is done. «The shop sold the bike lawfully, with a proper receipt.» The receipt is a clue that the sale followed the rules, so lawfully means in a way the law allows.
explain: Lawfully is a formal word. You will meet it in news reports, in legal writing and in wedding vows: «They were lawfully married in the church.» It is used a lot in phrases such as lawfully entitled, act lawfully and lawfully owned.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Try legally in place of lawfully: «The shop sold the bike legally, with a proper receipt.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Lawfully means in a way that is allowed by the law.
fact: Lawfully is another way of saying legally.
fact: Lawfully is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done: «She acted lawfully.»
fact: In formal language, lawfully can also mean properly allowed by rules or official authority.
fact: Lawful is the adjective: «a lawful choice» is one that the law allows.
exercise: «The farmer sold his land lawfully, after signing all the papers.» What does lawfully mean here? | Angrily, in a loud voice | Secretly, behind closed doors | Quickly and without thinking | *In a way the law allows
exercise: Which word is the adjective that lawfully comes from? | lawless | *lawful | lawyer | laws
exercise: «The guards acted lawfully when they searched the car.» What does this tell us? | They broke the law | They were very angry | They ran away quickly | *They followed the law
test: «The family lawfully owned the house for forty years.» What does lawfully mean in this sentence? | Very quickly | *In a way the law allows | With lots of noise | Secretly
test: Which phrase means the same as lawfully in its formal sense, properly allowed by the rules? | Carelessly | *Properly, by the rules | Quietly | Sadly
exam: «The council lawfully closed the road for repairs, as the rules allowed.» Why does the writer use lawfully? | To show it was raining | To show the road was very long | To show the workers were tired | *To show the closing was allowed by the rules
